text:
Returning 2 values can be more performant than returning one and assigning to another as an &quot;out parameter&quot;.<p>Eg:<p><pre><code>    typedef struct {
        bool has_value;
        int value;
    } option_int_t;

    option_int_t foo() {
        if (somecond) return (option_int_t){ true, somevalue };
        else return (option_int_t){ false };
    }

    bool bar(int *out) {
        if (somecond) {
            *out = somevalue;
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
</code></pre>
`foo` is slightly more efficient than `bar` on 64-bit SYSV, whose ABI supports 128-bit (&quot;two eightbytes&quot;) returns in hardware registers. `bar` requires at least one dereference to assign the result.<p>On other compilers this isn&#x27;t the case. MSVC only supports 64-bit returns and would use the stack for `option_int_t`. The caller allocates the space and an implicit pointer is passed in the first argument register (eg, `rcx`), and the same pointer is returned as the result.

If you do:<p><pre><code>    typedef struct {
        int status;
        float value;
    } div_result_t;

    div_result_t divide(float a, float b) {
        if (b = 0.0) return (div_result_t){ E_DIVZERO };
        return (div_result_t){ 0, a &#x2F; b };
    }
</code></pre>
On SYSV: The `status` gets returned in x0&#x2F;rax, and `value` gets returned in x1&#x2F;xmm0.<p>On MSVC: The caller allocates `sizeof(div_result_t)` on the stack, passes the the pointer to that space as an implicit first argument to `divide` in register x0&#x2F;rcx. The function returns the same pointer as its result in x0&#x2F;rax.<p>The latter is almost equivalent to you passing a pointer manually - but the compiler does it for you. In the former case, it&#x27;s actually more efficient to use the struct as we don&#x27;t even need to touch the stack - all arguments and returns are in registers (for structs up to two &quot;eightbytes&quot; - ie, 16-bytes).<p>IMO, if it intended to use automatic storage duration, and the size of the struct is small enough, returning by value is preferable - it performs better on SYSV, and basically automates what you would do manually on MSVC. If it is intended for manual storage duration, then yes, use pass and return by pointer explicitly.
